# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## Commands

This is a two-part app: a Vite/React frontend at the repo root, and an Express/Sequelize backend in `server/`. They run as separate processes.

**Frontend (repo root)**
```bash
npm install
npm run dev       # Vite dev server, http://localhost:5173
npm run build     # production build -> dist/
npm run preview   # preview the production build
```

**Backend (`server/`)**
```bash
cd server
npm install
node server.js    # http://localhost:4000 — no dev/start script is defined, run directly
```
The backend requires a `.env` file in `server/` (loaded via `dotenv`) with `DB_NAME`, `DB_USER`, `DB_PASSWORD`, `DB_HOST`, `DB_PORT` for the MariaDB/MySQL connection; see `server/db.js` for defaults. On startup it calls `sequelize.sync({ alter: true })`, so schema changes to the models in `db.js` are auto-migrated — there are no migration files.

**Tests**: there is no test runner wired up (`npm test` in either package is a stub). Verification is done with a handful of standalone Node scripts at the repo root (`test_security_isolation.js`, `test_area_permissions_isolation.js`, `test_sla_pause.js`) that hit a running local backend via `fetch`. Start the backend first, then run one directly, e.g.:
```bash
node test_security_isolation.js
```
**Lint**: no ESLint/Prettier config is present in this repo.

**Deployment**: `ecosystem.config.js` at the repo root defines the PM2 process(es) used in production. There is an automated deployment pipeline (Bin Manager–style) that pushes changes directly to the production server (`10.23.105.36`, hostname `VLC-OPSQL-PRD`) — production files are reachable from an admin workstation via the UNC/admin share `\\10.23.105.36\f$\VolcanDesk` (`backend/`, `frontend/`, `ecosystem.config.js`, `RUNBOOK.md`). Treat this as a live production environment: DB read reliability issues have previously occurred here (see below), and changes should be validated locally before pushing.

**Language rule**: the entire UI, alerts, validation messages, and system-generated text must stay strictly in Spanish. Any new user-facing string (frontend or backend-generated) should follow this convention.

## Architecture

**Client/server split**: `src/services/api.js` is the single fetch wrapper all data services go through; it hardcodes `API_BASE_URL = 'http://localhost:4000/api'` and attaches `x-user-id`/`x-user-role` headers read from `localStorage['tms_current_user']`. Every resource has a matching service module (`src/services/ticketService.js`, `userService.js`, `companyService.js`, `groupService.js`, `configService.js`, `flowService.js`, `categoryService.js`, `attachmentService.js`, `notificationService.js`) that wraps `api` calls for that resource — this is the layer to touch when adding/changing an endpoint's client usage.

**Auth model**: there is no session/JWT (despite `jsonwebtoken` being a listed backend dependency, it isn't used). The entire auth mechanism is the `x-user-id`/`x-user-role` headers set from `localStorage`. Server-side, `server/server.js` reads those into `req.user` via a global middleware, then gates routes with `requireAdmin` / `requireInternal`, plus a per-ticket `checkTicketAccess(ticket, user)` helper that layers in `ambito`, company match (for `client` role), and group membership (for internal roles, including `AreaAccesoExtra` grants, and now also *apoyo* (cross-group support) membership — see "Apoyos Solicitados" below). `RESTRICTED_TICKET_FIELDS` lists ticket fields only internal roles may set directly; `sanitizeTicketForRole` strips `activities` and private comments before a `client` sees a ticket. When changing ticket read/write behavior, these are the choke points to check together, not just the route handler.

**Data model** (`server/db.js`, all Sequelize models + associations in one file): `Company`, `User`, `Ticket`, `Comment`, `Config`, `Group`, `AreaAccesoExtra`, `TicketBloqueoHistorial`, `CampoCatalogo`, `ReglaFlujo`, `TicketCampoValor`. `Ticket` also has an `apoyos` JSON column (added for the cross-group support flow — see below) storing the history/state of support requests directly on the ticket row rather than in a separate table.

**Recurring bug pattern — `ticket.group` (legacy name) vs `ticket.resolver_group_id` (id, source of truth)**: `ticket.group` was originally a group *name* string and is still populated that way in some paths (manual/API-created tickets, older records); `resolver_group_id` is the actual group id and should always be preferred when it's set. Any group-membership check written as `allowedGroups.includes(ticket.group)` (or a Sequelize `{ group: { [Op.in]: allowedGroups } }` clause) without an `resolver_group_id ||` fallback will silently drop tickets that were routed to a group but still have a name (not an id) in `group`. This exact bug has recurred four times independently: `src/services/ticketService.js` (`getVisibleTickets`, `isGroupTicket`), `server/server.js` (`checkTicketAccess`, used by single-ticket GET/PUT), `server/server.js` (`GET /api/tickets`'s own separate inline `whereClause[Op.or]`), and `src/pages/Tickets.jsx` (`filteredTickets`'s role==='agent' branch, which re-filters client-side even though the backend already scoped the response). When adding any new group-visibility check, always compare against `ticket.resolver_group_id || ticket.group`, never `ticket.group` alone.

**Group semantics — resolver vs requester vs apoyo (three independent dimensions)**: a ticket has `area_origen_id`, the *requesting* group, independent of `resolver_group_id`/`group`, the *resolving* (owning) group (e.g. Operaciones can request a ticket that BI & Development resolves). A third, more recent dimension is the `apoyos` JSON list: one or more groups asked to *help* on the ticket without ever becoming the owner (see "Apoyos Solicitados" below). `isGroupTicket(t, groupIds)` in `ticketService.js` matches *either* the requester or resolver dimension — appropriate for "is this relevant to my group at all" (the Dashboard/Reports "Totales de mi grupo" toggle) — and now should also be considered against apoyo membership when deciding what counts as "relevant to my group." Anywhere meant to reflect a team's actual queue/workload — Kanban columns in `KanbanBoard.jsx`, the Dashboard/Reports admin "Ver grupo" filter, `Tickets.jsx`'s `deskRoleViewMatch('groupAttention', ...)` — must match `resolver_group_id || group` **or** an active apoyo grant for that group, not `isGroupTicket()` alone, or requester-only tickets bleed into the wrong team's view while apoyo tickets could disappear from the helping team's queue.

**Cross-group collaboration — "Apoyos Solicitados"**: this is the biggest structural addition after the original design. It lets one group ask another for help on a ticket *without* transferring ownership — the resolving group named in `resolver_group_id`/`group` always stays the owner of record to the end user. Mechanics: a "Solicitar apoyo a otro grupo" button in the ticket's top toolbar opens a modal to pick the target group and leave a message describing what's needed; this is persisted into the ticket's `apoyos` JSON column (`db.js`) rather than a separate table. A collapsible "Apoyos Solicitados" panel in the right-hand sidebar of `TicketDetailView.jsx` is visible to everyone involved (original group and apoyo group) and lets either side change the apoyo's status (Pendiente / En progreso / Resuelto). Tickets where your group has been requested as apoyo now surface automatically in `Atención > De mi grupo` (the visibility logic in `ticketService.js`/`Tickets.jsx` was extended to include apoyo membership, not just requester/resolver — see the group-semantics note above). Agents from the apoyo group were also granted the same internal-notes (private comments) read/write permission as the owning group, so the two teams can coordinate through `Comment`s the client/requester never sees; this permission layering lives alongside `checkTicketAccess` in `server.js`. Two bugs worth knowing about if you touch this area again: (1) the "Agente Asignado" field used to show as "Sin Asignar" for cross-group agents because of a lookup that only resolved within the viewer's own group — now always resolves from the server response; (2) querying tickets by apoyo group membership means filtering on a JSON column in MySQL/MariaDB via Sequelize, which needed specific query tuning to avoid full-table-scan slowness and, worse, a 500 error / tickets silently vanishing from the main list when the JSON predicate was written naively — if you add new queries against `apoyos`, test them against a realistically sized `Ticket` table, not just a handful of rows.

**Dynamic ticket fields (EAV-style)**: `CampoCatalogo` defines available custom fields (typed: texto/numero/fecha/hora/moneda/select/**archivo**); `ReglaFlujo` maps a ticket `tipo`/`categoria` combination to which fields apply and whether they're required; `TicketCampoValor` stores the actual per-ticket values. `GET /api/reglas-flujo/resolve` resolves the applicable rule for a given type/category. `CampoCatalogo.scope` distinguishes reusable `'flujo'` fields from `'base'` fields that are fixed per-module form fields. Two notable additions: (1) **dependent categories** — the "Categoría" select in ticket forms is now conditioned on the previously-selected "Tipo," resolved through the same `ReglaFlujo`/`CampoCatalogo` catalog, with a visual step-by-step guide (Paso 1/2/3) added to `Settings.jsx`/`FlowsConfigPage.jsx` to help admins configure the dependency correctly; (2) the `'archivo'` field type lets a dynamic field accept a file upload directly (not just the ticket-level attachments), and `TicketDetailView.jsx` renders these as clickable "pill" chips with an icon in the original ticket's request card instead of dumping raw base64/text, so downloads stay usable without breaking the layout.

**Group Administrator role scoping**: a "Group Admin" (Administrador de Grupo) is now strictly isolated to their own group — `Users.jsx`/`userService.js` (client) and the corresponding `server.js` route guards only let this role view, create, and manage users belonging to their own group. When a Group Admin creates a new user, the group field is no longer asked for — the new user is auto-assigned to the creator's own group, removing a whole class of misassignment errors — and Group Admins are blocked from editing the global "Grupos de Soporte" (support-group) assignment to avoid cross-area mistakes.

**Conversation view redesign (Freshdesk-style)**: the ticket chat in `TicketDetailView.jsx` was restructured so requester messages render in white cards and owner/agent messages in blue cards, making the speaker obvious at a glance. The old floating action button (FAB) for replying was removed in favor of a top toolbar with explicit "Responder" and "Nota Interna" actions. A chronological-ordering bug (messages appearing out of time sequence) was fixed so the thread always reads top-to-bottom correctly, and a privacy bug where private notes briefly flashed visible to unauthorized viewers before being hidden was patched. The "Información de la solicitud" card was reorganized to cleanly embed the dynamic (custom) fields inline and hide redundant fields, reducing visual clutter.

**Navigation/state persistence**: reloading the page (F5) used to drop the agent back to the app's initial screen; this is fixed so the current page/section (and the open ticket, where applicable) is restored after a reload instead of being lost — check `App.jsx`/`Tickets.jsx` (and whatever persistence mechanism they use, e.g. `localStorage` or URL state) before changing top-level navigation.

**Email notifications**: real email alerts are now sent to ticket participants on relevant events. Template rendering bugs that produced literal `undefined` text in the email preview were fixed, and relative-time formatting was normalized to read naturally (e.g. "Hace 3 horas"). There's now an explicit rule governing who gets notified when a new Volcán Desk (internal) ticket is created with no agent assigned yet — check this logic (likely in `server.js` alongside ticket-creation and the notification service) before changing ticket-creation or assignment flows, since it's easy to silently break the "who gets notified" rule.

**Production stability**: earlier deployment work also fixed a class of bugs where the production database appeared not to load existing users, groups, or tickets — caused by overly aggressive security/locking filters on bulk-extraction queries. If a "nothing loads" report comes in against production, check bulk list/extraction endpoints for overzealous `WHERE`/lock clauses before assuming it's a data problem.

**Attachments**: uploaded as base64 inside JSON payloads and decoded server-side (`processAttachments` in `server.js`), not multipart. Storage path resolution tries `G:\MariaDB\attachments` first, falls back to the UNC path `\\10.23.105.36\G$\MariaDB\attachments`, then to `server/attachments` locally — this reflects a specific on-prem file share and matters if attachment storage ever needs to change. Files are served statically from `/api/uploads`. (Dynamic-field file uploads, added later, are a related but separate mechanism — see "Dynamic ticket fields" above.)

**Ticket list caching**: `src/services/ticketService.js` keeps a module-level `cachedTickets` array, populated only by `loadTicketsFromServer()`. `App.jsx` calls this once on initial mount; after that it goes stale unless a page proactively re-fetches. `Tickets.jsx` self-polls every 8s (`setInterval(..., 8000)` calling `loadTicketsFromServer()` + `refreshTickets()`), and `Dashboard.jsx`/`Reports.jsx` now do the same. Any new page reading `getVisibleTickets()`/`getTickets()` needs the same polling (or at least a fetch on mount) or it will silently show whatever was cached at app startup.

**Voltik vs. Volcán Desk (two workspaces, one Ticket table)**: `ticket.ambito` (`'externo' | 'interno'`) is set once at creation and never changes. `externo` tickets are the customer-facing flow ("Voltik": company + contact); `interno` tickets are the cross-department internal helpdesk flow ("Volcán Desk"). Both live in the same `Ticket` table and share most catalogs, but every list/query must filter by `ambito` — nothing should mix the two. The active workspace on the frontend is tracked in `src/context/WorkspaceContext.jsx` (persisted to `localStorage['tms_workspace']`, values `'voltik'` / `'volcan_desk'`) and only changes UI framing (labels, accent color, which tab set is shown) — it is not itself a query filter. `User.modulo` and `Group.modulo` similarly pin an internal user/group to one module permanently at creation. Full spec: `docs/specs/prompt-voltik-volcandesk-separacion.md`; related specs (dynamic flows, escalation, Kanban, client-reply indicator, catalog separation) are the other files under `docs/specs/`.

**Frontend structure**: no router library — `App.jsx` swaps pages via a `currentPage` state string in a switch statement. `src/pages/` holds one component per top-level page (Dashboard, Tickets, Kanban, Reports, Users, Settings, plus `FlowsConfigPage`/`FormFieldsConfigPage` for admin config), `src/components/` holds shared UI (ticket table/detail view, Kanban board, modal, multiselect, rich text editor, notification inbox, contact autocomplete), `src/context/WorkspaceContext.jsx` holds the active-workspace state described above, `src/utils/helpers.js` holds shared utilities.

**README note**: `README.md` describes an older localStorage-only, no-backend version of this app. That's no longer accurate — the app now requires the `server/` Express+MariaDB backend described above; treat the backend/API details in this file as authoritative over the README.
